				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				77,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(Grove): Align header spacing in architecture documentation\n\nAdjust whitespace in `Documentation/GitHub/Architecture.md` to match the project's typography standards for headers. Remove inconsistent double spaces following the section emoji and ensure single space separation between the title text and the emoji character.\n\nThis update brings the visual hierarchy of the Architecture document in line with recent standardization efforts in `README.md` and other Grove documentation files, ensuring consistent rendering across all markdown headers.",
			"SHA": "addcaa59e23588b1a06922e8f93b8f2aeb87bfdc"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-15T23:35:51Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				77,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(Grove): Enhance architecture documentation with standardization and emojis\n\nRefactor `Documentation/GitHub/Architecture.md` to align with project documentation standards and improve visual hierarchy. Apply consistent Markdown backticks to all technical terminology (e.g., `Grove`, `Rust`, `WASM`, `WASMtime`, `Mountain`, `VS Code`, `Cargo`) throughout the document.\n\nAdd section-specific emojis (e.g., 📋, 🏗️, 🔗) to headers to improve navigation and visual distinction. Rewrite the overview section from a paragraph to a bulleted list for better readability. Update the \"Related Documentation\" links to use absolute GitHub URLs targeting the `Current` branch. Ensure the `HostBridge` and `TransportStrategy` code examples retain proper formatting.\n\nThese changes ensure the documentation style matches other Land elements like Common and Mountain, making the Grove architecture clearer for contributors.",
			"SHA": "c4a32904cade6f06817cc9fe98d893fa6d65f79e"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-15T22:31:45Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				77,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(Grove): Add Mermaid diagram for five-layer architecture\n\nInsert a Mermaid sequence diagram into `Documentation/GitHub/Architecture.md` visualizing the Grove native extension host structure. The diagram details the five distinct layers: HOST (lifecycle management), WASM (WASMtime runtime and memory), TRANSPORT (gRPC/IPC/WASM strategies), API (VSCode.rs surface), and PROTOCOL (Spine connection).\n\nIt also illustrates the communication flow between the Protocol layer and the Mountain gRPC server, complementing the existing text-based architectural overview.",
			"SHA": "e1a0b044e1a5b2b23a43b0a0cf981ed65d79d5f0"
		}
